            <div xml:id="d167">

                <pb facs="0325" n="211" xml:id="pg211"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">700.0011 R 34/–: Circular telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Herrick</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-01-02T13:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 2,
                                1923—1 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>The Department of State today made the following announcement to the Press:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>The German Ambassador, on behalf of his Government, recently submitted to
                            the Secretary of State a proposal to the effect that the Powers
                            interested in the Rhine, to wit, France, Great Britain, Italy and
                            Germany, should “solemnly agree among themselves and promise the
                            Government of the United States that they will not resort to war against
                            each other for a period of one generation without being authorized to do
                            so by a plebiscite of their own people.”</p>
                        <p>It was deemed inadvisable to transmit the proposal to the Governments
                            named unless it appeared that it would be favorably considered by the
                            French Government. On making informal inquiry of the French Government,
                            the Secretary of State was informed that that Government could not view
                            the proposal with favor as such an arrangement could not be made under
                            the provisions of the French Constitution.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Repeat to London, Berlin, Rome, Lausanne, Brussels.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
